---
/* ============================================================================
 *  CompanyScene — "Every tool in its place. Nearly."
 *
 *  WHERE IT IS USED: the Company page, in the statement band under the header —
 *  the /our-mission layout, statement left, illustration right. Adopted
 *  2026-09-14 on Harshit's sign-off, and moved here from components/preview/ at
 *  the same time. /company-preview was parked in the same commit; un-park it
 *  (drop the underscore) to rehearse a change to this scene in context.
 *
 *  It replaced WorkshopScene, which kept the joke but failed Gate 1b on both
 *  counts: 29.7% bare cream against a floor of 60, and 7.2% dark against a
 *  ceiling of 5, because its board was a wall over 54% of the frame. That
 *  component was deleted on Harshit's instruction once this one had shipped;
 *  it is recoverable from commit 9aeddcf, but the numbers above are the reason
 *  not to.
 *
 *  ── THE COMPOSITION IS THE 404's ────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  A tall object with the cat ON TOP of it, props either side, a cloud above.
 *  Stacking is the only arrangement that fits a board big enough for legible
 *  tools AND a cat at full 20.4% size: side by side they need over 200 units and
 *  the props have nowhere left to stand.
 *
 *  Two earlier attempts are worth not repeating. A 100x72 board on a bench was
 *  quiet, gate-passing and unreadable — at 352px it came out 84px wide with 7px
 *  tools, and you could not see anything was missing. A 136x142 board with the
 *  cat on the floor in front read fine but squashed the cat to a lump.
 *
 *  ── COLOUR: THE SCREWDRIVER SETS IT ─────────────────────────────────────────
 *  Harshit, 2026-09-14: "tools has no color except screwdriver, to make it
 *  simple follow the color theme of the screw driver for all the tools."
 *  He was describing a real fault. Every tool was filled DEVICE — the SAME
 *  colour as the board behind it — so only a navy contour separated a tool from
 *  its own backing, and the painted outlines (FUR_SHADE, darker than the board)
 *  read as the solid objects. Exactly backwards.
 *
 *  So the screwdriver's two colours became the whole kit's: one colour for
 *  whatever you hold, KEYS for whatever does the work. Both are darker than the
 *  board, so a tool reads as an object and its ghost recedes behind it. DEVICE is
 *  now the board and the holes punched through tools, and nothing else.
 *
 *  The held colour was orange for one revision and is now NAVY — the monitor
 *  screen's own #1B2A4A — because seven orange tools fought the one orange band
 *  on the mug, which is the site's only accent. See the GRIP const below.
 *
 *  ── THE JOKE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  Six slots, two rows of three, on a 160-wide board: columns at 172/224/276,
 *  which leaves ~20 units of cream between neighbours. The first cut spaced them
 *  34 apart and the second row touched — a 32-wide hammer and a 32-wide tape
 *  measure cannot share a 34-unit pitch, and the ghosts' 2-unit offset closed
 *  what little was left. Slot two is bare, because that screwdriver is up top
 *  under a paw, and it stays bare for the whole loop: the caption is true at
 *  every frame, which it was not when the tool went home to its hook.
 *
 *  ── THE LOOP ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  16 seconds, on Harshit's brief: the cat sleeps with the screwdriver lying
 *  beside it · it wakes · it plays with the screwdriver, walking it toward the
 *  edge · it knocks it off · it goes back to sleep · the screwdriver comes back
 *  up to the cat, where it started · repeat. It returns TO THE CAT, not to its
 *  hook — the loop closes on the frame it opened on.
 *
 *  ── THE RESTING STATE IS THE CAPTION'S MOMENT ───────────────────────────────
 *  Every SVG *attribute* describes the frame the caption is about: eyes open,
 *  screwdriver up top under the paw, slot two bare. Reduced motion gets that;
 *  so do the gate scripts, which render attributes and ignore CSS. The tool's
 *  attribute transform and the 0%/26% keyframe are deliberately the same pose.
 *
 *  ── THINGS THAT MUST STAY TRUE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  · The GROUND is y=300 and the board's base sits on it. Nothing crosses it.
 *  · A tool is FILLED (KEYS body, GRIP where you hold it, navy contour). Its
 *    painted outline is FLAT (one fill, no stroke, no grip), offset 2 left /
 *    2 down. That contrast is the only thing telling a viewer which slot is
 *    empty — never stroke the outlines and never colour them.
 *  · The props sit in the site's one order: cloud top left, then grass · MUG ·
 *    subject · PLANT · grass. Set by Harshit on 2026-09-14 so every scene is the
 *    same picture with the middle swapped out. QuietScene (the 404) is the
 *    reference; this scene's mug and plant are its paths verbatim, each on one
 *    static translate, so matching a future change is one number.
 *  · Each silhouette is declared ONCE as a const and used TWICE: flat for the
 *    ghost, filled for the tool. Grips and holes are overlays on top of it, so a
 *    tool can never drift off its own outline.
 *  · The screwdriver is drawn ONCE, vertical, at its slot, and is ALWAYS
 *    transformed away from it. Its fill-box centre is (224, 163); every pose
 *    below rotates about that.
 *  · Breathing, the tail, the steam, the wind and the cloud's drift are NOT
 *    here. They are shared by every scene and live in
 *    src/styles/scene-motion.css; this svg opts in with `data-scene`.
 * ========================================================================== */
interface Props {
  /** Small mono status label above the art, as on QuietScene. Omit for none. */
  chip?: string;
  /** Caption below the art. Omit for none. */
  caption?: string;
  /** Unique per instance — the title id must not collide. */
  id: string;
  class?: string;
}
const { chip, caption, id, class: className = '' } = Astro.props;
const titleId = `company-scene-${id}`;

/* ── QuietScene's weights and palette, deliberately. ── */
const NAVY = '#1B2A4A';
const ENVLINE = '#CBD5E1';
const W_SUBJ = 2;
const W_FACE = 1.8;
const W_FINE = 1.4;

const GROUND = '#E3EAF3';
const DEVICE = '#C9D9EC'; // the 404 rack's own blue — the board, and holes in tools
const KEYS = '#8FA8C8'; // every working end: jaws, heads, shafts, blades
const FUR = '#FFFFFF';
const FUR_SHADE = '#B9C4D2'; // and the painted outlines: flat, never stroked
const LEAF_A = '#7DD87D';
const LEAF_B = '#4EB86B';
const POT = '#FCDCCF';
const GRASS = '#3FAE5F';
const MUG = '#F7B9A3';
const CLOUD = '#FFFFFF';
const ORANGE = '#F24E1E'; // the mug's band, and nothing else in this scene
/* Every part of a tool you hold. Harshit, 2026-09-14: "orange tools is breaking
   the colour harmony. make it dark navy instead of orange just like the computer
   screen." It IS the computer screen — CareersScene's SCREEN is #1B2A4A, the
   same navy every contour in every scene is drawn in. So a grip is a contour
   thickened into a solid, which is why it sits on the board without shouting. */
const GRIP = '#1B2A4A';

/* QuietScene's cat, verbatim. */
const CAT_BODY = 'M180 128 C178 110 190 98 208 98 C226 98 242 106 244 128 Z';
const CAT_BACK =
  'M180 128 C178 110 190 98 208 98 C218 98 227 101 233 107 C214 104 192 112 186 128 Z';
const HEAD_SHADE = 'M226 110 C229 104 235 102 239 104 C233 105.5 228 108 226 113 Z';

/* ── The six slots. Columns 172 / 224 / 276 on a board spanning 144–304; rows at
      136–190 and 214–268. Each silhouette below is the ghost AND the tool body;
      grips, holes and details are drawn over it. ── */

/* row 1 · ring spanner — a ring on a shaft, so it cannot be read as a torch */
const SPANNER =
  'M161 147 C161 141 166 136 172 136 C178 136 183 141 183 147 C183 152 180 156 176 158 L176 188 C176 190 168 190 168 188 L168 158 C164 156 161 152 161 147 Z';
const SPANNER_GRIP = 'M168 170 L176 170 L176 188 C176 190 168 190 168 188 Z';

/* row 1 · pliers — one head and two legs, gripped on the lower half only. In
   navy the full-length legs read as a pair of trousers; real pliers are sleeved
   from about the midpoint down, and that is what breaks the shape back up. The
   two grips are the exact lower halves of the legs, split at t=0.5 by de
   Casteljau, so they sit on the curve rather than near it. */
const PLIER_HEAD = 'M269 136 L283 136 L285 152 L276 158 L267 152 Z';
const PLIER_LEFT = 'M271 160 C268 170 266 180 266 190 L272 190 C272 180 274 170 277 162 Z';
const PLIER_RIGHT = 'M281 160 C284 170 286 180 286 190 L280 190 C280 180 278 170 275 162 Z';
const PLIER_LEFT_GRIP = 'M267.4 175 C266.5 180 266 185 266 190 L272 190 C272 185 272.5 180 273.4 175.3 Z';
const PLIER_RIGHT_GRIP =
  'M284.6 175 C285.5 180 286 185 286 190 L280 190 C280 185 279.5 180 278.6 175.3 Z';

/* row 2 · hammer */
const HAMMER =
  'M160 214 L184 214 C187 214 188 216 188 219 L188 223 C188 226 187 228 184 228 L177 228 L177 265 Q177 268 172 268 Q167 268 167 265 L167 228 L160 228 C157 228 156 226 156 223 L156 219 C156 216 157 214 160 214 Z';
const HAMMER_GRIP = 'M167 236 L177 236 L177 265 Q177 268 172 268 Q167 268 167 265 Z';

/* row 2 · tape measure — case, blade straight down, end hook across the foot.
   The first cut ran the blade down and then right, into an L, and at 28px that
   reads as a boot. Symmetry is what makes it a tape measure. */
const TAPE_BODY =
  'M215 214 L233 214 C238 214 240 217 240 222 L240 236 C240 241 238 244 233 244 L215 244 C210 244 208 241 208 236 L208 222 C208 217 210 214 215 214 Z';
const TAPE_TONGUE = 'M219 244 L229 244 L229 260 L235 260 L235 268 L213 268 L213 260 L219 260 Z';

/* row 2 · coiled cable, hung as a hank with a tie round it. The first cut was a
   fat oval with a bar across, which read as an eye. Narrow is what makes it
   cable: the hole has to look like two strands side by side, not a ring. */
const COIL_OUTER =
  'M276 214 C285 214 289 221 289 232 L289 250 C289 261 285 268 276 268 C267 268 263 261 263 250 L263 232 C263 221 267 214 276 214 Z';
const COIL_INNER =
  'M276 226 C280 226 281 230 281 234 L281 248 C281 252 280 256 276 256 C272 256 271 252 271 248 L271 234 C271 230 272 226 276 226 Z';

/* Slot two's tool: drawn ONCE, vertical, in its slot. Its bounding box is
   x 215–233, y 136–190, so its centre is (224, 163) — every transform below
   rotates about that. The flat handle top and the quadratic skirt are chosen so
   the box is exactly those numbers; a cubic would undershoot and move it. */
const SD_HANDLE = 'M215 136 L233 136 L233 159 Q233 164 224 164 Q215 164 215 159 Z';
const SD_SHAFT = 'M221.5 164 L226.5 164 L226 182 L222 182 Z';
const SD_BLADE = 'M218.5 182 L229.5 182 L229.5 190 L218.5 190 Z';
---

<figure class:list={['company', className]}>
  {
    chip && (
      <p class="chip">
        <span class="chip-dot" aria-hidden="true" />
        {chip}
      </p>
    )
  }

  <svg
    class="scene"
    data-scene
    viewBox="13 18 420 330"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby={titleId}
    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
  >
    <title id={titleId}>
      A workshop shadow board with six tools, each hanging over its own painted outline. One
      outline is bare: a cat sitting on top of the board has that screwdriver under its paw.
    </title>

    <g fill="none" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
      <!-- ── Cloud: QuietScene's own, top left, as on every scene. The translate
           is the viewBox offset, so it sits exactly where the 404's does
           relative to the frame. On a static parent because the drift is
           animated and a CSS transform replaces a transform attribute. ── -->
      <g transform="translate(13 18)">
        <path
          class="cloud"
          d="M60 80 C55 80 54 71 61 69 C62 60 74 57 80 63 C86 56 99 59 100 68 C108 68 110 79 104 80 Z"
          fill={CLOUD}
          stroke={NAVY}
          stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
        />
      </g>

      <!-- ── Ground: the 404's own line, and it reaches neither edge ── -->
      <ellipse cx="224" cy="302" rx="100" ry="7" fill={GROUND} />
      <path d="M52 300 H394" stroke={ENVLINE} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />

      <!-- ══ THE BOARD — the hero object, the job the rack does on the 404.
               160 x 180, widened from 136 so the second row stops touching. ══ -->
      <rect
        x="144"
        y="120"
        width="160"
        height="180"
        rx="4"
        fill={DEVICE}
        stroke={NAVY}
        stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
      />
      <g fill={KEYS}>
        <circle cx="153" cy="129" r="1.5" />
        <circle cx="295" cy="129" r="1.5" />
        <circle cx="153" cy="291" r="1.5" />
        <circle cx="295" cy="291" r="1.5" />
      </g>

      <!-- ══ THE RACK. Everything hung on the board sits inside this one static
               translate, which buys the bottom row its margin: the slots are
               authored at 136–190 and 214–268, which left 16 units of clear
               board above the first row and 32 below the last. Shifting the lot
               down 8 makes that 16 above and 24 below, without touching a single
               tool path. The screwdriver is NOT in here — it never visits its
               slot, so it keeps its own coordinates. ══ -->
      <g transform="translate(0 8)">
      <!-- The painted outlines. FLAT — one fill, no stroke, no orange — because
           that is the only thing that tells a tool apart from the space where
           one should be. Laid 2 left / 2 down: a real shadow board is
           mis-registered too. -->
      <g fill={FUR_SHADE} transform="translate(-2 2)">
        <path d={SPANNER} />
        <path d={PLIER_HEAD} />
        <path d={PLIER_LEFT} />
        <path d={PLIER_RIGHT} />
        <path d={HAMMER} />
        <path d={TAPE_BODY} />
        <path d={TAPE_TONGUE} />
        <path d={COIL_OUTER} />
        <!-- SLOT TWO. Nothing hangs here for the whole loop. The caption
             depends on this outline being visible. -->
        <path d={SD_HANDLE} />
        <path d={SD_SHAFT} />
        <path d={SD_BLADE} />
      </g>

      <!-- hooks: two rows of three -->
      <g stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE}>
        <path d="M172 128 L172 136" />
        <path d="M224 128 L224 136" />
        <path d="M276 128 L276 136" />
        <path d="M172 206 L172 214" />
        <path d="M224 206 L224 214" />
        <path d="M276 206 L276 214" />
      </g>

      <!-- row 1 · ring spanner: steel body, orange grip, a hole you see through -->
      <path d={SPANNER} fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <circle cx="172" cy="147" r="5" fill={DEVICE} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE} />
      <path d={SPANNER_GRIP} fill={GRIP} />
      <path d="M168 170 H176" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE} />

      <!-- row 1 · slot two: nothing. The screwdriver is up top. -->

      <!-- row 1 · pliers: steel jaws and arms, sleeved grips below the midpoint -->
      <path d={PLIER_LEFT} fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <path d={PLIER_RIGHT} fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <path d={PLIER_LEFT_GRIP} fill={GRIP} />
      <path d={PLIER_RIGHT_GRIP} fill={GRIP} />
      <path d={PLIER_HEAD} fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <circle cx="276" cy="157" r="2.4" fill={NAVY} />

      <!-- row 2 · hammer: steel head, orange grip -->
      <path d={HAMMER} fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <path d={HAMMER_GRIP} fill={GRIP} />
      <path d="M167 236 H177" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE} />
      <path d="M156 222 H188" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE} />

      <!-- row 2 · tape measure: orange case, steel blade -->
      <path d={TAPE_TONGUE} fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <path d={TAPE_BODY} fill={GRIP} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <circle cx="224" cy="229" r="8" fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE} />

      <!-- row 2 · a coiled cable: steel hank, orange tie round the middle -->
      <path d={COIL_OUTER} fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      <path d={COIL_INNER} fill={DEVICE} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE} />
      <rect
        x="262"
        y="235"
        width="28"
        height="9"
        rx="2"
        fill={GRIP}
        stroke={NAVY}
        stroke-width={W_FINE}
      />
      </g>

      <!-- ══ CAT — QuietScene's own drawing, on top of the board ══ -->
      <g transform="translate(14 -8)">
        <path
          class="tail"
          d="M182 126 C170 128 164 120 170 113 C174 108 181 110 181 116"
          stroke={NAVY}
          stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
        />
        <g class="breathe">
          <path d={CAT_BODY} fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
          <!-- mis-registration: the back shading, laid inside and short of its contour -->
          <path d={CAT_BACK} fill={FUR_SHADE} />
          <path d={CAT_BODY} fill="none" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
          <g stroke={ENVLINE} stroke-width={W_SUBJ}>
            <path d="M193 102 Q197 107 195 112" />
            <path d="M203 99.5 Q207 104 205 109" />
          </g>
        </g>
        <ellipse cx="213" cy="124.5" rx="8.5" ry="3.5" fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
        <g class="head">
          <path d="M227.5 108 L228 96 L237 103.5 Z" fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
          <path d="M241 103.5 L248 97 L247.5 110 Z" fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
          <circle cx="236" cy="116" r="12" fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
          <path d={HEAD_SHADE} fill={FUR_SHADE} />
          <!-- asleep: QuietScene's own closed, contented eyes -->
          <g class="eyes-shut" opacity="0" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FACE}>
            <path d="M228 114 Q231 117 234 114" />
            <path d="M239 114 Q242 117 245 114" />
          </g>
          <!-- awake, pupils low and right: looking at what it has taken -->
          <g class="eyes-open">
            <circle cx="232" cy="115" r="1.9" fill={NAVY} />
            <circle cx="243" cy="115" r="1.9" fill={NAVY} />
            <path d="M228.5 111 Q231 109.3 233.5 111" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FACE} />
            <path d="M239.5 111 Q242 109.3 244.5 111" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FACE} />
          </g>
          <path d="M236 119.5 L236 121" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FACE} />
          <g stroke={ENVLINE} stroke-width={W_FINE}>
            <path d="M227 119 L222.5 118" />
            <path d="M245 119 L249.5 118" />
          </g>
        </g>
      </g>

      <!-- ══ THE SCREWDRIVER. Drawn once, vertical, in its slot; the attribute
               transform is the pose the caption is about — up top, horizontal,
               blade out past the edge — and the 0%/26% keyframe repeats it
               exactly. Everywhere else it goes is another transform, so the tool
               and its own painted outline can never drift apart. ══ -->
      <g class="tool" transform="translate(72 -52) rotate(-90 224 163)">
        <path d={SD_HANDLE} fill={GRIP} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
        <path d={SD_SHAFT} fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE} />
        <path d={SD_BLADE} fill={KEYS} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_FINE} />
      </g>
      <ellipse class="paw" cx="282" cy="108" rx="6.5" ry="3.5" fill={FUR} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />

      <!-- ══ MUG — LEFT, between the grass and the board. QuietScene's paths on
               one static parent: static because the steam is animated and a CSS
               transform replaces a transform attribute rather than composing
               with it. ══ -->
      <g transform="translate(-8 8)">
        <g class="steam" stroke={ENVLINE} stroke-width={W_SUBJ}>
          <path class="wisp wisp-1" d="M103 262 C100 256 108 252 105 246" />
          <path class="wisp wisp-2" d="M112 262 C109 256 117 252 114 246" />
        </g>
        <path
          d="M96 268 L96 286 Q96 292 102 292 L114 292 Q120 292 120 286 L120 268 Z"
          fill={MUG}
          stroke={NAVY}
          stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
        />
        <rect x="97" y="278" width="22" height="5" fill={ORANGE} opacity="0.9" />
        <path d="M96 273 H120" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
        <path d="M120 275 C130 275 130 287 120 287" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      </g>

      <!-- ══ PLANT — RIGHT, between the board and the grass. Leaves AND pot are
               QuietScene's paths untouched, inside one static parent, so the
               whole plant moves as a number rather than as re-typed geometry.
               The swaying group carries no transform of its own. ══ -->
      <g transform="translate(11 8)">
        <g class="leaves">
          <path
            d="M330 274 C322 258 326 244 332 240 C338 246 338 262 330 274 Z"
            fill={LEAF_A}
            stroke={NAVY}
            stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
          />
          <path
            d="M330 274 C318 266 312 254 314 248 C322 250 330 262 330 274 Z"
            fill={LEAF_B}
            stroke={NAVY}
            stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
          />
          <path
            d="M330 274 C342 266 348 256 346 250 C338 252 331 263 330 274 Z"
            fill={LEAF_A}
            stroke={NAVY}
            stroke-width={W_SUBJ}
          />
        </g>
        <path d="M314 274 L318 292 H342 L346 274 Z" fill={POT} stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
        <path d="M312 274 H348" stroke={NAVY} stroke-width={W_SUBJ} />
      </g>

      <!-- ══ GRASS — both sides, as on the 404 ══ -->
      <g stroke={GRASS} stroke-width="3.5">
        <g class="grass grass-b">
          <path d="M62 300 C61 292 64 285 69 280" />
          <path d="M69 300 C69 292 71 286 75 282" />
          <path d="M75 300 C75 293 74 288 72 284" />
        </g>
        <g class="grass">
          <path d="M372 300 C371 292 374 285 379 280" />
          <path d="M379 300 C379 292 381 286 385 282" />
          <path d="M385 300 C385 293 384 288 382 284" />
        </g>
      </g>
    </g>
  </svg>

  {caption && <figcaption class="line">{caption}</figcaption>}
</figure>

<style>
  .company {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.85rem 0.35rem 0.7rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: #fff;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgb(15 23 42 / 0.04);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-navy-700);
  }
  .chip-dot {
    width: 0.375rem;
    height: 0.375rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: #16a34a;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgb(22 163 74 / 0.14);
  }
  .scene {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    /* the cap every scene carries, so one cat is one size wherever it appears */
    max-width: 22rem;
    height: auto;
  }
  .line {
    max-width: 30ch;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-navy-900);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* ── THE LOOP ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       16 seconds, and the three animations below are one gesture split three
       ways, so their percentages must stay in step:

            0%   0.0s   asleep · the screwdriver lying beside the cat
           22%   3.5s   it wakes
           27%   4.3s   the paw comes down on the handle
        33–47%   5–7.5s three bats, walking the tool toward the edge
           54%   8.6s   the push, and it tips
           56%   9.0s   falling, end over end
           58%   9.3s   it lands on the floor
           65%  10.4s   back to sleep, the screwdriver still down there
        82–88%  13–14s  it comes back up TO THE CAT, where it started
          100%  16.0s   loop, on the frame it opened on

       It never returns to its hook: slot two is bare at every frame, so the
